findings.body:
**No test pins the fsmonitor override.**

The hook runs outside the worker's sandbox, and the worker writes the checkout's config. The `-c core.fsmonitor=false` argument is the only thing stopping the hook from running a `core.fsmonitor` command the worker configured.

`fsmonitor` appears nowhere in `tools/hooks-rs/tests/`. Deleting the argument therefore leaves the suite green, which contradicts the PR's "26 of 26 mutants caught".

The test below would go after `the_request_id_ignores_an_ignored_file` in `tests/worker_permission.rs`. It has not been run. Its precondition assert checks that the fixture really triggers the command, so keep that assert even if the fixture needs adjusting (for example, adding `git update-index --fsmonitor`):

```rust
// The worker writes the checkout's config, and the hook runs outside its sandbox.
#[test]
fn the_files_listing_runs_no_fsmonitor_command_the_checkout_names() {
    use std::os::unix::fs::PermissionsExt;
    let lead = Lead::new("wp-id-files-fsmonitor");
    let repo = checkout(&lead);
    let marker = lead.tmp.path().join("ran");
    let script = lead.tmp.path().join("fsmonitor.sh");
    write(&script, &format!("#!/bin/sh\ntouch {}\n", marker.display()));
    std::fs::set_permissions(&script, std::fs::Permissions::from_mode(0o755))
        .expect("make the script executable");
    git(&repo, &["config", "core.untrackedCache", "true"]);
    git(&repo, &["update-index", "--untracked-cache"]);
    git(&repo, &["config", "core.fsmonitor", script.to_str().expect("a UTF-8 path")]);
    git(&repo, &["ls-files", "--others"]);
    assert!(marker.exists(), "the fixture does not trigger the fsmonitor command");
    std::fs::remove_file(&marker).expect("remove the marker");
    assert!(!lead.bash(&repo, "bash build.sh").id().is_empty());
    assert!(!marker.exists(), "the hook ran the checkout's fsmonitor command");
}
